{\cite{Boardman-1970}}\label{Boardman-Corollary}
For any $d \geq 1$, let $A \subseteq \mathbb R^d$ be a set with the property
\begin{equation}  \limsup_{R \rightarrow \infty} \sup_{x \in \mathbb R^d} \frac{\lambda_d(A \cap Q(x;R))}{R^d} > \frac{1}{2}, \quad Q(x;R) := x + [0, R]^d. \label{Boardman-condition} \end{equation} 
Then $\Delta(A)$ contains all distances, i.e. $\Delta(A) = [0, \infty)$.

 \label{Sparse-all-distances-corollary}
For $d \geq 2$ and $s$ sufficiently close to $d$, there exists a totally disconnected Borel set $A \subseteq \mathbb R^d$ with the following properties:
\[ \dim_{\mathbb H}(A) = s < d \quad \text{ and } \quad \Delta(A \setminus B(0;R)) = [0, \infty) \text{ for every $R > 0$.} \]

