\begin{equation}  \limsup_{R \rightarrow \infty} \frac{\lambda_d(A \cap B(0;R))}{\lambda_d(B(0;R))} > 0, \label{Bourgain-condition} \end{equation} 
where $B(x;R)$ denotes a ball of radius $R$ centred at $x$. Then $A$ contains all sufficiently large distances; namely, there exists $R_0 = R_0(A) > 0$ with the property that for every $R \geq R_0$, there exist $x, y \in A$ with $|x-y| = R$.

